  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 956 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nMOISTURE DIMINISHES THROUGH THE WEEKEND, BUT STORMS CONTINUE:\n\nThe western CONUS low begins a steady trek through the Great \nBasin and, eventually, the Intermountain West. This begins to\npush the pool of powerful moisture out of the region and begins\na slow drying out across the West Slope. On Friday though, we \nwill still be holding onto quite a bit of moisture with PWAT's \nsomewhere around 200% of normal give or take. We will reside \nunderneath the entrance of a weak jet streak as well, so there \nis some evidence that Friday will actually produce some \nrelatively strong storms. Cloud cover is still expected to be \ndense in the morning, so that will limit storm coverage and \nupdraft strength, but we've already seen a couple days this week\nexceed expectations, so don't rule out a few instances of \npowerful outflows, sub-severe hail, and localized heavy rainfall\ncapable of exacerbating flash flooding concerns. According to \nCAM's, these types of storms are most likely in the far \nnorthwest and southeast corners of the CWA during the afternoon\nand evening, but other regions across the CWA could still see \nisolated impacts.\n\nBy Saturday, PWAT's should be firmly below 200% of normal. This\nis enough moisture to keep some isolated storms going, but \ncertainly we will see coverage continue to diminish. By Sunday, \nmoisture will be near-normal, or maybe even a touch below \nnormal. This comes as the trough's axis crosses over the CWA. \nStill, flow around the base of the trough looks to be powerful, \nand as such, some orographic showers could develop. Impacts \nwould likely be minimal with these storms though.\n\nMOISTURE QUICKLY RETURNS:\n\nThe relatively dry period won't stick around for too long as \nanother low follows behind the first, moving into the \nsouthwestern CONUS on Monday. There's plenty of uncertainty \namong ensembles on how much moisture will be drawn up through \nthe southwesterly flow. The GFS Ens keeps the plume mostly to \nthe southeast while the ECMWF Ens draws the plume more directly \noverhead. In either case, this round of moisture is expected to \nbring about another round of showers and thunderstorms. It'd \ntake some time for the moisture to arrive, so Monday will likely\nstill be on the quiet side with the arrival of stormy skies \noccurring Tuesday or Wednesday. By this point though, models are\ndiverging by quite a bit as evident through cluster analysis. \nSome evidence suggest the low will persist and swing through \nour region, which would support more powerful storm development,\nwhile other guidance appears to more quickly break down the \ntrough before it can make its way further inland. Once we gain \nmore clarity on these details we'll be able to speculate on \npotential impacts.",
